{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And He blotted out.</b> It is a binyan qal form (לשון ויפעל), not nifal (לשון ויפעל), and it is conjugated like וַיִּפֶן and וַיִּבֶן — every noun whose final letter is he, such as בנה, מחה, קנה: when vav-yod is placed at its head, it is vocalized with a chirik under the yod." "<b>Only Noah.</b> Noah alone — this is its plain sense. And a midrashic reading: he was sighing and spitting blood from the burden of the animals and the beasts. And there are those who say that he was late with the lion's food and it bit him, and concerning him it is said: ״הֵן צַדִּיק בָּאָרֶץ יְשֻׁלָּם״ (Proverbs 11)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "Rashi's grammatical terminology: he distinguishes the qal (active, his 'לשון ויפעל') from nifal (passive, his 'לשון ויפעל') by the consonant following the yod — a dagesh in the next consonant (qal) vs. its absence (nifal). Here the dagesh in the mem of וימח marks it qal."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T17:26:16.283538044Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}